WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.300
Hello! Today, for the third time on my channel,

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.500
I have as SSRF vulnerability in Gitlab. This time,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.300
however, it was found in the external component called Grafana.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.900
It was reported by Justin Gartner and he got $12,000 for it.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.200
Links to the report as well as to his blog post and his talk

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.300
about the topic are in the description.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.600
Enjoy the video!

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.800
Let's start with grafana.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.800
It's a software used for monitoring and creating graphs.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.900
with analytics data. And it's open source, which means

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.600
it's a little bit easier to look for vulnerabilities, because

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.300
we can see the source code. And the researcher noticed the

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.500
root /avatar/:hash

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.800
where hash is the parameter. And after receiving such

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.400
requests grafana took the hash and made the request to

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.700
gravatar website.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:02.000
It's used for storing avatars. The URL for this request.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.000
Is created like this:

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:09.700
The first part is hard-coded and the second part is our hash.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.100
What's important here is that the hash is everything after

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.700
the last slash of the first request, which means it includes any

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.700
parameters after the question mark. Then there is question

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.400
mark appended and value of reqParams, which are hard-coded

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:37.300
parameters d, size and r. So we can only control the last

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.600
part of the path and the parameters, which means that we are

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:47.100
far from SSRF vulnerability. Unless we can find an open

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.300
redirect vulnerability on a gravatar website and then we

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.100
could use it to redirect the server to the internal location

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.200
achieving our server site request forgery.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.900
For this purpose a gravatar documentation about the default

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:10.699
URL might be very useful. The D or default parameter that

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:15.600
you saw earlier is used when gravatar doesn't find the image

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.000
on his own server.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:22.700
It can be some predefined value like retro, but it can also

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:27.000
be an URL which is very promising when you're looking for

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.100
open redirects.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:34.100
But when you provide the URL you get redirected to yet another domain. 

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:39.800
This is the way that you would tell this domain where

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.300
to look for your image.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.635
It would try to go to this location and give you back the image.

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:56.000
But if it would be on the domain bp.blogspot.com

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.700
instead of fetching this image for you,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.800
It would redirect you to that domain. But you could exploit

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.400
this functionality by using bp.blogspot.com as the

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.400
path achieving an open redirect. So we can use this chain

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.600
of redirects to make the server send the request to specified

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.100
by us host.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.300
However, there is still one problem.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.200
There will always be that bp.blogspot.com part in the

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:35.100
path. But this is easy to resolve. We can just introduce yet

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:40.000
another redirect, this time to our server, and on our server

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.300
we can host the code that will strip this part and send the

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:49.200
one last open redirect to the arbitrary host and arbitrary path.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.000
Chaining this all together the final exploit URL would

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.000
look like this:

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.100
However, if we would send it like this then grafana would

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.100
think that only this part is the hash.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.620
So we need to remember to URL encode some values, sometimes

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.820
even more than once.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.300
On Gitlab it was exploitable by requesting a standard AWS metadata

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:24.500
endpoint which usually allows to take over the whole server.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.000
And that's it! If you're interested in more ways.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:29.000
of exploiting

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.500
This grafana vulnerability, check out Justin's blog post

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.000
or his talk about the topic.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.300
They are linked in the description, but for now, thanks for

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.400
watching. Happy hunting and goodbye!

